*Streptococcus pneumoniae* is a priority pathogen for new antibiotics. Developing a new drug
takes a decade; finding one among drugs that already exist takes less. We asked whether a model
could pick repurposing candidates well enough to be worth a lab's time.

<Stats items={[
  { value: '36,352', label: 'compounds', sub: '1,849 active' },
  { value: '6,747', label: 'drugs screened', sub: 'Drug Repurposing Hub' },
  { value: '9 / 11', label: 'active in vitro', sub: 'IC50 ≤ 0.4 µg/mL' },
  { value: '60 pM', label: 'most potent hit', sub: 'thiostrepton' }
]} />

Published open access, so every number below is checkable:

> Fitch, J. S.; **Ariadi, M. D.**; Kwun, M. J.; Howells, L.; Hernandez-Hernandez, S.;
> Croucher, N. J.; Ballester, P. J. "Artificial Intelligence-Guided Phenotypic Drug Repurposing
> Against *Streptococcus pneumoniae*." *Advanced Science*, 2026.
> [doi:10.1002/advs.76959](https://doi.org/10.1002/advs.76959) ·
> [full text on Europe PMC](https://europepmc.org/article/MED/42745629)

My working repository for the modelling is
[github.com/mdhyaksa/pneumoccusVS](https://github.com/mdhyaksa/pneumoccusVS). The paper's own
data-availability statement points at the first author's repository, which is the canonical
artefact for the publication.

## The prospective result

Eleven drugs went to the lab. Nine reduced growth of *S. pneumoniae* R6.

<Legend items={[
  { label: 'Active in vitro', series: 1 },
  { label: 'Not active at the tested concentration', censored: true }
]} />

<Figure
  title="Measured IC50 of the 11 prospectively selected drugs"
  caption="Log scale, lower is more potent. Shown as a dot plot rather than bars: on a log axis there is no zero baseline, so bar length would imply a magnitude it cannot carry. Hollow markers with a caret are lower bounds — those two did not clear the threshold."
  source="Advanced Science 2026, Table 2. Assay: S. pneumoniae R6, 3 biological replicates per concentration.">
  <HBar items={ic50} mode="dot" scale="log" min={0.00008} max={0.6}
        ticks={[0.0001, 0.001, 0.01, 0.1]}
        format={(v) => v < 0.001 ? '0.0001' : v < 0.01 ? '0.001' : v < 0.1 ? '0.01' : '0.1'}
        axisLabel="IC50 (µg/mL)" />
  {#snippet table()}
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Drug</th><th>IC50 µg/mL</th><th>Mechanism</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {#each ic50 as d}
        <tr><td>{d.label}</td><td>{d.display}</td><td>{d.note}</td></tr>
      {/each}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  {/snippet}
</Figure>

Six of the eleven were cephalosporins. Only levofloxacin was already known to work against this
organism. Thiostrepton held its potency against three multidrug-resistant strains, at 120 pM.

## Where the models disagreed

This is the part worth dwelling on. Ranking the eleven tested drugs by model score against their
measured potency:

<Figure
  title="Rank correlation with measured potency (Spearman ρ, n = 11)"
  caption="The chemical language model ordered the compounds roughly correctly. The random forest did not — its correlation is indistinguishable from chance at this sample size."
  source="Advanced Science 2026, Table 3. Two-tailed. Authors' caveats: n = 11 is small, and selection favoured compounds the deep models ranked highly.">
  <HBar items={rho} max={1} ticks={[0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1]}
        format={(v) => v.toFixed(2)} axisLabel="Spearman ρ" labelWidth={168} />
  {#snippet table()}
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>ρ</th><th>p</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {#each rho as d}
        <tr><td>{d.label}</td><td>{d.display}</td><td>{d.note}</td></tr>
      {/each}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  {/snippet}
</Figure>

But the forest was not simply worse. It ranked levofloxacin 7th where both deep models buried it
around 120th; it put thiostrepton — the most potent compound found — at 115th, where the deep
models ranked it 1st; dactinomycin was 7th for MoLFormer and 192nd for the forest.

**No single model would have found every active.** The conclusion is not that one architecture
won, but that they fail in different directions — an argument for consensus screening, and
against reading a leaderboard too literally.

## The split mattered more than the model

A molecule that resembles its training set is easy to score, and scoring it well says little
about a genuinely new compound. Random splits over chemical data produce exactly that illusion.

<Flow steps={[
  { label: 'Hold out the structural outliers', detail: '4,812 molecules with maximum Tanimoto similarity below 0.5 to anything else — the compounds a model has least right to be confident about become the test set' },
  { label: 'Cluster what remains', detail: 'UMAP (Jaccard) then agglomerative clustering; K = 26 chosen by silhouette over a sweep from 3 to 50' },
  { label: 'Leave one cluster out', detail: 'One ensemble member per held-out cluster, so every member is validated on a scaffold group it never saw' },
  { label: 'Tune on PR-AUC', detail: 'With a positive class under 6%, accuracy is uninformative and ROC-AUC is flattering', kind: 'gate' }
]} note="Positive rates across the 26 clusters range from 0% to 57% — a warning about what a random split would have mixed together." />

Three model families ran over the same splits: a random forest on 2048-bit Morgan fingerprints;
a 26-model directed message-passing graph network; and a five-model ensemble on a frozen
pre-trained chemical language model, where ~80% of its 55M parameters never updated and only the
head was trained.

<Figure
  title="Retrospective PR-AUC on the held-out structural outliers"
  caption="49 actives in the test set. The intersection of the two best models buys precision at the cost of recall — the useful trade when the downstream cost is a wet-lab assay. ROC-AUC sat at 0.95–0.98 for every row including the weakest, which is why it is not the metric shown."
  source="Held-out set: 4,812 molecules, Tanimoto < 0.5 to any other compound.">
  <HBar items={prauc} max={0.7} ticks={[0, 0.2, 0.4, 0.6]}
        format={(v) => v.toFixed(1)} axisLabel="PR-AUC" labelWidth={150} />
  {#snippet table()}
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>PR-AUC</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {#each prauc as d}<tr><td>{d.label}</td><td>{d.display}</td></tr>{/each}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  {/snippet}
</Figure>

## The data, and one judgement call

1,849 compounds with measured activity came from ChEMBL and PubChem, mostly from assays on
erythromycin- and penicillin-resistant strains. The 34,503 inactives are mostly *presumed*
inactive, taken from a published *S. aureus* screen — an assumption, not a measurement. The paper
justifies it on a pneumococcal screening hit rate around 0.6% and a 0.80 disk-diffusion
correlation between the two species across 47 antibiotic–concentration pairs, and ablates it by
retraining without those negatives.

## Interpretability

SHAP over fingerprint bits, substructure highlighting to map important bits back onto atoms, and
Monte Carlo tree search to extract minimal active subgraphs scored by the MoLFormer head. β-lactam
and quinolone scaffolds surfaced as expected — mostly a sanity check that the models learned known
chemistry.

One note for anyone opening the repository: the notebook named `pneumococcus_causal.ipynb`
contains no causal inference. It is attribution work, and the name oversells it.

## Stack

PyTorch, Lightning, scikit-learn, imbalanced-learn, DeepChem, Chemprop, RDKit, transformers,
Ray Tune, SHAP, UMAP. Trained on Imperial's PBS cluster on RTX6000 GPUs, ensembles as array jobs.
